CORE
plain.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ first argument decides: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [over.match.best]/2.1, [over.ics.rank]/3.3: two user-defined
conversion sequences are compared only when they use the same conversion
function or constructor.  The front end added the rank of the argument's
conversion to the constructor parameter (here the null pointer conversion
of 0 to int *) to the user-defined conversion's rank, so `0 -> unspec' lost
to `0 -> count' and eq(count, count) was selected (bin53).  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept this program (-std=c++98), and it fails only its WRONG
assertion.
